# Design review — DP `picture-batch-001`, S001

I downloaded `S001.mp4` from DP release `picture-batch-001`, verified SHA-256 `e7e247e987a2b10a31d52572897330eb51250e2af77f8ab0fafc6badc58935d8`, decoded it with FFmpeg 7.0.2-static to `framemd5` without errors and inspected frames at 00:02, 00:07, 00:12, 00:17 and 00:23, shown in `reviews/s001-sampled-frames.jpg`. All 619 frames decoded; 398 of frames 60–559 are distinct; first and last frames match (both black per DP's checked release). The full-frame worn olive plate patchwork persists over the sampled interval with a subtle pan and no visible design jump. This **picture plate** meets `design/opening-wall.md` and the approved S001 key frame at DP e074bb8. It contains no title lettering and no sound; that is by assignment, not approval of a finished SC01 cut.

I compared source frames at 00:02, 00:07, 00:12, 00:17 in `design-opening-v1` to the released plate. The remake wall has deeper 3D patina and similar ochre-green patchwork, leaves room for titles, and differs visibly from the original pixels. The source title was pale, tall and irregular/serif-like, physically integrated with tiles. DP `evidence/s001-title-diagnostic.png` is only a legibility test; its plain modern sans-serif **is not the design-approved title style**. Editor owns the actual lettering and must make it bone-white, narrow/irregular with a subtle tile-integrated painted/raised appearance and camera tracking, readable ≥8 s per director's SC01. Judge that only in a rendered editorial cut; do not infer it from this text-free clip.

This review is about design continuity in one shot, not lock approval or D1–D5 QC of a cut. The reference-only original stills never enter the picture. The authoritative 127-ID source index is `design/shot-reference-index.csv` and release `design-shot-reference-v2`.
